# RTL Coding Standards

## Module Structure
All new modules should be written in SystemVerilog. Reference [rtl/chip.sv](mdc:rtl/chip.sv) for an example of proper module structure.

## Naming Conventions
- Module names: snake_case (e.g., `memory_controller`)
- Parameters: UPPER_SNAKE_CASE (e.g., `MEMORY_SIZE`)
- Signals: snake_case
- Clock signals: prefix with `clk_`
- Reset signals: prefix with `rst_`

## Code Style
- Use 2-space indentation
- Include comprehensive module headers
- Use blocking assignments (`=`) for combinational logic
- Use non-blocking assignments (`<=`) for sequential logic
- Always include reset handling for sequential elements

## Example Module Header
```systemverilog
// Module: memory_controller
// Description: Controls access to main system memory
// Parameters:
//   - ADDR_WIDTH: Address bus width
//   - DATA_WIDTH: Data bus width
// Ports:
//   - clk_i: System clock
//   - rst_i: Active-high reset
// Author: Your Name
// Date: YYYY-MM-DD
```

## Memory Map
See [src/memory.cfg](mdc:src/memory.cfg) for the complete memory layout:
- 0x0000-0x00FF: Zero Page
- 0x0100-0x01FF: Stack
- 0x0200-0x0FFF: Main RAM
- 0x1000-0x1FFF: Text buffer
- 0x2000-0x2FFF: Sprite memory
- 0x3000-0x3FFF: I/O space
- 0x4000-0x4FFF: Memory-mapped I/O
- 0xFFFA-0xFFFF: Interrupt vectors
